    "answer": {
      "short": "$P=(1,-3)$.\n\nMatch the derivative to the gradient of the given line, then check which candidate lies on the line.",
      "worked": "The tangent is $y=-x-2$, so its gradient is $-1$. The curve has derivative $y'=3x^2-4$. At a point of tangency,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}3x^2-4&=-1\\\\x^2&=1\\\\x&=\\pm1.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSubstitution into the cubic gives $(1,-3)$ and $(-1,3)$. The given line has $y=-3$ at $x=1$ and $y=-1$ at $x=-1$, so $P=(1,-3)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
